WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.780
why doesn't the width of your own car appear anywhere in the mathematics of parallel parking?

00:05.520 --> 00:08.960
Look at mathematician Simon Blackburn's exact parking formula.

00:09.480 --> 00:13.280
It uses your turning radius, your wheelbase, your front overhang,

00:13.660 --> 00:15.920
and the width of the parked car in front of you.

00:16.340 --> 00:18.900
Your own car's width? Completely missing.

00:19.540 --> 00:20.140
Here is why.

00:20.620 --> 00:23.140
In a single smooth sweep on a wide road,

00:23.460 --> 00:28.100
the only pinch point is your curbside front corner barely missing the car in front.

00:28.100 --> 00:33.220
Making your own car wider pushes the outside edge closer to your turning center

00:33.220 --> 00:36.000
without changing that front corner's path at all.

00:36.580 --> 00:40.980
In real life, of course, a wider car still steals room on narrow streets

00:40.980 --> 00:43.180
or when you have to shuffle back and forth.

